Found while measuring karr #105 (sops 3.13.3 at /tmp/sops). Independent of it,
and NOT fixed by ADR 0026, which touches unencrypted slots only.

An ENCRYPTED slot holding a YAML `.inf` is stored differently by the two
implementations, and neither side fails:

  plaintext  v: .inf
  sops -e    v: ENC[...,type:float]   plaintext +Inf
  File::SOPS v: ENC[...,type:str]     plaintext .inf

Both documents verify their own MAC and both are read by the other side, so
this is a value divergence rather than a compatibility failure:

  sops -e       -> sops -d        v: .inf         (a float)
  File::SOPS -e -> sops -d        v: ".inf"       (a string)

Measured for all twelve %GO_CONSTANT spellings, both directions, exit 0
throughout. Reading is already correct in both directions: File::SOPS decrypts
sops's type:float +Inf to a real Perl infinity (bit-identical, pinned in t/39),
and sops reads our type:str back as the string it is.

MECHANISM: the same one as karr #105. YAML::XS hands `.inf` back as a POK-only
string, so detect_type says str and the value is encrypted verbatim; go-yaml
resolves the float before sops ever types the leaf. ADR 0026 repairs this at
parse time for a document that carries a sops section, which an encrypted slot
of a PLAINTEXT document being encrypted does not -- the walk is deliberately
gated off there (ADR 0026, "Rejected alternatives", last entry but one).

WHY IT IS LOW: nothing fails, nothing is lost, and a round trip through either
implementation alone is stable. What differs is the type label and therefore
what a caller of the OTHER implementation gets back for that key -- a float
where the first writer meant a float, or a string.

Fixing it means letting the karr #105 walk run on plaintext documents too,
which was rejected in ADR 0026 for a measured reason (it replaces ADR 0013's
good error message with the non-finite guard's misleading one) -- so this
probably waits on karr #113. Wire lane. Needs the sops binary to prove.
MESSUNG ABGESCHLOSSEN, KEIN CODE GEAENDERT. sops 3.13.3 /tmp/sops.
ENTSCHEIDUNG: in der Wire-Lane ist NICHTS ZU TUN, und das ist die Antwort, kein
Aufschub. Die Divergenz kostet gemessen nichts, sie entsteht nicht in dieser
Schicht, und ihre Reparatur ist HINTER karr #122 eingereiht, nicht davor.

